\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Counting sign changes on a cycle}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Along a cycle #{\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z}, a real component can
pass through zero at a vertex or change sign between two adjacent
samples. After a crossing from negative to positive, it must
cross back before completing a circuit. In two dimensions a
Fourier block can have two real coefficients, and both must
vanish at the same momentum. Knowing their signs at the corners
of a square leaves their values between the corners unspecified.
How can the neighboring samples determine a sign for such a
common zero?}
